## Concept
HTTP/2 suffers from head-of-line blocking at the TCP level - a single lost packet blocks all streams. QUIC solves this by using UDP as the transport protocol and implementing reliability, congestion control, and stream multiplexing at the application layer.

## Implementation
```go
// QUIC stream independence
for _, stream := range conn.Streams() {
    go func(s quic.Stream) {
        // Each stream processes independently
        data, err := io.ReadAll(s)
        // Lost packets only affect this stream
        handleStreamData(s.ID(), data)
    }(stream)
}

// Connection-level recovery
conn.HandleLostPackets(func(packetID uint64) {
    // Selective retransmission
    if shouldRetransmit(packetID) {
        conn.RetransmitPacket(packetID)
    }
})
```

## Trade-offs
- **Pros**: Stream independence, faster recovery, reduced latency
- **Cons**: Higher CPU usage, UDP firewall issues, complex implementation

## Why This Is Asked
Tests hardware offload, OS UDP paths, and QoS interplay in a multi-region fabric, focusing on telemetry reliability under bursts with no app changes.

## Key Concepts
- NIC RSS hashing and RX queues
- UDP tail drops and sockstat counters
- VXLAN/EVPN MTU and fragmentation
- Per-tenant rate limiting and edge QoS

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple UDP burst tester (Node.js)
const dgram = require('dgram');
const client = dgram.createSocket('udp4');
const TARGET = {host: 'collector.example', port: 9000};
let sent = 0, burst = 1000;
function sendNext(){
  if (sent >= burst) { client.close(); return; }
  client.send(Buffer.from('x'), 0, 1, TARGET.port, TARGET.host, () => {});
  sent++;
  if (sent % 10 === 0) setImmediate(sendNext);
  else setTimeout(sendNext, 0);
}
sendNext();
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you differentiate RSS-induced drops from MTU fragmentation issues?
- Which measurements would you automate for a non-disruptive rollback if a mitigation fails?
